insert into public.strongs_drift_notes (number, note) values
('G1577', 'Ekklēsia meant an assembly: a body of people called together. In Acts 19 the same word names the crowd in the Ephesus theater and the city''s lawful assembly, and there the KJV has "assembly" (Acts 19:32, 39, 41). In the New Testament it usually means a community of believers, never a building. Tyndale translated it "congregation"; the KJV translators were instructed to keep the traditional word "church."'),
('G0086', 'Hadēs is the Greek name for the realm of the dead; the Septuagint uses it for Hebrew Sheol. The KJV renders it "hell" ten times and "grave" once (1 Corinthians 15:55, where most modern editions read "death"). Many readers take it as the intermediate state of the dead, distinct from Gehenna, the place of final judgment; others see it as including punishment (Luke 16:23).'),
('G1067', 'Geenna is the Greek form of the Hebrew Gē Hinnōm, the Valley of Hinnom outside Jerusalem, linked with child sacrifice (Jeremiah 7:31) and later with judgment. Jesus uses it for final punishment (e.g. Matthew 10:28). The KJV renders both Geenna and Hadēs as "hell," so the distinction is lost in English. Most Catholic, Orthodox and Protestant teaching reads it as eternal punishment; some evangelicals and Adventists read it as final destruction, and a smaller minority as purifying.'),
('H7585', 'Sheol is the Hebrew Bible''s name for the place or state of the dead, where righteous and wicked alike go (Genesis 37:35; Ecclesiastes 9:10). The KJV renders it "grave" 31 times, "hell" 31 times and "pit" 3 times, so one Hebrew word appears under three English words. Clearer hopes of resurrection and judgment appear in later texts such as Daniel 12:2 and grew in Jewish and Christian thought.'),
('G5020', 'Tartaroō, used only in 2 Peter 2:4, means "to cast into Tartarus," in Greek thought the deepest part of the underworld, where rebellious beings were confined. The KJV''s "cast them down to hell" merges it with Hadēs and Gehenna. Here it describes angels who sinned, held for judgment.'),
('G0907', 'Baptizō means to dip, immerse or wash, and can describe ordinary washing: in Mark 7:4 and Luke 11:38 the KJV renders it "wash." English Bibles, following the Latin, have transliterated rather than translated it, as "baptize," which leaves the question of mode open. Traditions differ on whether the word requires immersion or also allows pouring or sprinkling.'),
('G4716', 'Stauros meant an upright stake or pole, and was the ordinary word for the Roman instrument of crucifixion. Ancient sources describe several forms: a single stake, or a stake with a crossbeam (the patibulum, which the condemned often carried). Most historians and Christian traditions hold that Jesus died on a cross-shaped stauros; a minority, notably Jehovah''s Witnesses, render it "torture stake."'),
('G3957', 'Pascha is the Passover. The KJV renders it "passover" 28 times and "Easter" once, in Acts 12:4, where Herod waits until after the feast. Earlier English Bibles such as Tyndale''s used "Easter" more often. Most modern translations read "Passover" here; some defenders of the KJV argue "Easter" was deliberate.'),
('G0026', 'Agapē is the New Testament''s usual word for love. Its depth comes from context, such as God''s self-giving in 1 John 4:9–10, more than from the word itself; the Septuagint uses it even of Amnon''s love for Tamar (2 Samuel 13:15). The KJV renders it "love" 86 times and "charity" 27 times, including throughout 1 Corinthians 13, following the Latin caritas. In 1611 "charity" chiefly meant Christian love, though it could already mean almsgiving; today the giving sense dominates, so most modern translations read "love."'),
('G3340', 'Metanoeō means to change one''s mind, and in the New Testament a turning of the whole person toward God. The Vulgate''s paenitentiam agite (Matthew 3:2), which can mean "repent" or "do penance" (the Douay-Rheims reading), became a Reformation flashpoint: Erasmus and Luther stressed inner change, while Catholic teaching holds that conversion of heart comes first and is expressed in outward penance. Catholic, Orthodox and Protestant traditions all hold that repentance involves both a changed heart and its fruit (Matthew 3:8).'),
('G1343', 'Dikaiosynē covers both "righteousness" and "justice": English splits one Greek word into two. What Paul means by "the righteousness of God" (Romans 1:17) has been central to Christian debate: God''s own justice, a right standing God credits to believers (stressed in Lutheran and Reformed teaching), a righteousness God works in them (stressed in Catholic and Orthodox teaching), or God''s covenant faithfulness. Translations choose "righteousness" or "justice" depending on the reading.'),
('G4102', 'Pistis means faith, trust or faithfulness, and sometimes proof or a pledge. English separates "faith" (trusting) from "faithfulness" (being reliable); Greek uses one word. Scholars debate whether pistis Christou (Galatians 2:16) means faith in Christ or Christ''s own faithfulness. The KJV''s "the faith of Jesus Christ" can be read either way; many modern translations read "faith in Jesus Christ."'),
('G5485', 'Charis means favor, kindness or a gift freely given, and also the gratitude that answers it: sometimes "thanks," as in 1 Corinthians 15:57. In the Greco-Roman world it named the bond between benefactor and recipient; New Testament writers use it for God''s unearned favor. All traditions agree that grace begins as God''s free gift. Catholic and Orthodox teaching stresses grace as God''s transforming life, given especially through the sacraments; Protestant teaching stresses favor received by faith, though many Protestants also call sacraments means of grace.'),
('G3056', 'Logos means word, speech, account or reason. In John 1 it names the one who "was with God" and "was God," drawing on the Old Testament''s creative word and wisdom of God, and on Greek uses of logos for the rational order of the world. "Word" captures the speech sense but not the "reason" sense, which is why some writers leave it untranslated as Logos.'),
('G2098', 'Euangelion means good news. In the Roman world it could announce an emperor''s victory, birth or accession: the Priene inscription (9 BC) calls Augustus''s birthday the beginning of "good news" for the world. The Greek of Isaiah uses the related verb for news of God''s coming reign (Isaiah 40:9; 52:7). By the second century the word also named the written accounts of Jesus; English "gospel" comes from Old English gōdspel, "good tidings."'),
('G2889', 'Kosmos first meant order or arrangement, then the ordered universe, then humanity, and in John often humanity set against God (John 15:18). The KJV renders it "world" everywhere but once ("adorning," 1 Peter 3:3), so "God so loved the world" (John 3:16) and "love not the world" (1 John 2:15) look alike in English, though the Greek word is used in different senses.'),
('G0165', 'Aiōn means an age, a long period of time. The KJV often renders it "world": "the end of the world" (Matthew 28:20) is literally "the completion of the age." Phrases like eis tous aiōnas ("unto the ages") become "for ever." Readers sometimes take "world" as the physical earth where the Greek is speaking of time.'),
('G0166', 'Aiōnios, from aiōn ("age"), is rendered both "everlasting" and "eternal" in the KJV, sometimes in the same verse (Matthew 25:46). Most traditions read it as unending, noting that the same verse uses it for both punishment and life. Many scholars also stress its sense of "belonging to the age to come," describing the kind of life as well as its length; a minority, notably universalists, argue it need not mean endless for punishment.'),
('H2617', 'Chesed is loyal, steadfast love: kindness rooted in commitment, often within a covenant. No single English word fits. The KJV uses "mercy" most often, along with "kindness" and "lovingkindness," first found (as two words) in Miles Coverdale''s 1535 Bible. Modern translations try "steadfast love," "faithful love" or "unfailing love."'),
('H3068', 'YHWH is God''s personal name (Exodus 3:14–15). Out of reverence, Jewish readers say Adonai ("Lord") instead of pronouncing it, and the KJV follows this by usually printing "LORD" in capitals for YHWH, distinct from "Lord" for Adonai; where the two stand together it prints "Lord GOD." The form "Jehovah," used a few times in the KJV, combines YHWH''s consonants with Adonai''s vowels; scholars generally think it was pronounced "Yahweh."'),
('H7307', 'Ruach means wind, breath and spirit: one Hebrew word where English needs three. In Genesis 1:2 the KJV reads "the Spirit of God moved upon the face of the waters"; some translations read "a wind from God." In Ezekiel 37 the word moves through all three senses as the dry bones receive breath.'),
('G4151', 'Pneuma, like Hebrew ruach, means wind, breath or spirit; John 3:8 plays on this ("the wind bloweth… so is every one that is born of the Spirit"). The KJV uses both "Holy Ghost" and "Holy Spirit" for the same Greek phrase; "ghost" was then an ordinary word for spirit, as in "gave up the ghost." Modern translations generally use "Holy Spirit."'),
('H5315', 'Nephesh means a living being, life, breath or self, and sometimes appetite or throat. In Genesis 2:7 the man "became a living soul": the whole person, not a part within him. Scholars broadly agree the word itself does not mean an immortal part. Catholic, Orthodox, most Protestant and traditional Jewish teaching affirms that the person survives death; Seventh-day Adventists and some others hold that the dead sleep until the resurrection.'),
('G1228', 'Diabolos means slanderer or accuser. It usually names the devil, but it can also describe people: "slanderers" in 1 Timothy 3:11 and "false accusers" in Titus 2:3. The KJV also renders a different word, daimonion, as "devils"; modern translations call those "demons" and keep "the devil" for diabolos.'),
('G1140', 'Daimonion means a spirit or demon. The KJV renders it "devil" or "devils," the same English word it uses for diabolos (the devil), so the many "devils" of the Gospels are different beings from "the devil." In Acts 17:18 it keeps its older Greek sense of a deity: "a setter forth of strange gods."')
